{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_architecture_performance_invariants", "label": "Non-negotiable performance invariants", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L6-L9", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "These are the reason the project exists. Every feature must respect them, and a feature that cannot is cut rather than accommodated."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_redraw_only_on_damage", "label": "Invariant 1: redraw only on damage", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L11-L14", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The event loop sits in ControlFlow::Wait with no animation ticker and no continuous render loop, so an idle window costs 0% CPU. If a feature needs a steady frame clock, it is the feature that is wrong."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_bounded_caret_blink", "label": "The one bounded timer: caret blink", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L16-L20", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The caret blinks for ten seconds after the last keystroke and then goes solid, so the editor blinks while you work and wakes for nothing once you stop. Anything else wanting a clock gets the same treatment - bounded, self-cancelling, and justified in this file."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_idle_cpu_measurement", "label": "How to measure idle CPU", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L22-L34", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Two different mistakes both look exactly like a broken invariant: top makes the window it samples redraw and reports one to three percent of its own making, and a pointer resting over a focused window still delivers events. Use a ps CPU-time delta plus the event count over the same window: zero events with non-zero CPU is a real bug, events with CPU is the editor doing its job."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_viewport_bounded_layout", "label": "Invariant 2: lay out only what is visible", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L36-L39", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Shaping and drawing are proportional to the viewport, never to the document: pulling the ~60 lines a frame needs out of the rope costs 13 us whether the file holds 60 lines or three million."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_rope_residency", "label": "The document is fully resident at 1.15x", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L41-L47", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "What viewport-bounded layout does not buy, and an earlier draft of this file wrongly claimed it did: a 143 MB file is 164 MB of rope. Only the viewport is shaped - it is not the case that only the viewport is loaded."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_virtual_buffer", "label": "Virtual buffer over a memory map (unearned)", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L45-L47", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Making only-the-viewport-loaded true needs a virtual buffer over a memory map, which is a real project and not a milestone we have earned yet; it is also the only way to reduce the rope's 1.15x."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_no_index_of_our_own", "label": "Invariant 3: no index of our own", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L49-L52", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "We never walk, parse, or cache the whole project. Symbols come from the language server; text search streams through ripgrep's engine and keeps only the matches on screen. This is the single biggest reason mainstream editors idle at 1GB and we do not."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_one_draw_call", "label": "Invariant 4: one draw call for the text plane", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L54-L56", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The editor body is a character grid, so the whole screen is one instanced quad draw against one glyph atlas. Panels are the same primitive, not a widget tree."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_glyph_atlas", "label": "Glyph atlas", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L111-L126", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A single-channel texture holding rasterised glyphs keyed by char, face and size; 94 rasterised ASCII glyphs and the font system are the 11 MB of baseline that is actually ours."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_lazy_language_servers", "label": "Invariant 5: language servers are separate, lazy and killable", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L58-L61", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Spawned on the first buffer of that language, terminated when the last one closes. Their memory is theirs: rust-analyzer will use gigabytes and that is its business, not ours, while our own process stays in tens of megabytes."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_nothing_loaded_until_opened", "label": "Invariant 6: nothing is loaded until opened", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L63-L65", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Workspace open reads the directory listing of the root. Not the files, not the git objects, not the tree."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_measured_baseline", "label": "Measured memory baseline", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L66-L94", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "63 MB AppKit before a line of our code runs, 10 MB for the window, 3 MB for wgpu, 11 MB for the font and atlas stack. The floor is AppKit's, not ours, and is not reducible from inside the process; the same editor as a terminal UI would idle around 10 MB, against 500 MB to 1 GB for a mainstream Electron editor."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_crate_layout", "label": "Crate layout", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L95-L109", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Split for compile-time isolation and to keep editing logic pure: core has no I/O, no GPU and no async, and each later crate stays a leaf so touching cursor logic never recompiles wgpu."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_ascii_fast_path", "label": "ASCII fast path", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L111-L122", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A code editor is overwhelmingly a monospace ASCII grid, and exploiting that is most of the performance story: the printable ASCII range is rasterized once into the atlas, and drawing a screenful is filling an instance buffer with (cell_x, cell_y, glyph_index, fg, bg) and issuing one draw - no shaping, no per-line layout, no allocation."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_shaping_slow_path", "label": "cosmic-text shaping slow path", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L121-L123", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "CJK, emoji, combining marks and RTL go through full cosmic-text shaping and the shaped result is cached per unique run: rare in source code, correct when it happens."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_swash_rasterization", "label": "swash glyph rasterization", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L125-L126", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Using cosmic-text's swash backend gives real hinting and font fallback without owning a font stack."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_milestones", "label": "Milestones M1-M7", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L128-L146", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Each one ends at something usable, not a demo; since M7: the four goto requests, references, call hierarchy, rename, formatting, code actions with codeAction/resolve, context menus, in-place naming and a file clipboard with its own undo stack."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_awaiting", "label": "Awaiting carries the request's own arguments", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L148-L152", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A language-server reply is only meaningful next to the question, so Awaiting carries the name a rename asked for and the buffer revision a formatting reply has to match. A reply that arrives after the buffer has moved on is dropped rather than applied to text it does not describe."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_lsp_follow_up", "label": "Lsp::follow_up for two-step requests", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L154-L160", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A call hierarchy has to name the symbol before it can ask who calls it, and a code action may arrive without the edits it implies, so the second request is issued from the first one's reply. follow_up takes no document position, because by then the question is about something the server already named."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_rename_workspace_edit", "label": "Rename edits open buffers and closed files differently", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L162-L165", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Opening every touched file as a tab would be tidier for undo and worse to use: a rename across forty files would leave forty tabs."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_inline_edit_row", "label": "The tree's inline edit row is a real row", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L166-L168", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Inserted into rows where the result will appear, so drawing and hit-testing need no notion of it; the only place that knows is the one line that draws its label."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_file_blame", "label": "FileBlame keeps git blame --porcelain's shape", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L170-L176", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Line blame reads the whole file once, not a line at a time: the annotation follows the cursor, so a subprocess per line would mean one per keypress on the way down a file - invariant 1 in everything but name. git blame --porcelain already sends a commit's details only the first time its sha appears, so FileBlame stores commits once and an index per line."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_preview_glyph_sizes", "label": "Size::{Body, Large, Huge} instead of a number", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L177-L183", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A uniform grid is what lets a screenful of text cost one instance per cell, so the atlas does not take a number: it takes an enum, measures each size on first use, and caches glyphs under (char, face, size). A heading then occupies two grid rows rather than a fractional height, which keeps the preview's model - a line is a scroll step, and it sits on the grid."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_fenced_code_syntax", "label": "A throwaway Syntax per fence", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L185-L189", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Nothing is kept: a fence is tens of lines, the result is cached with the rendered preview, and a parser per code block would cost more than re-running this on the edit that invalidates it."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_run_boundary_wrapping", "label": "A run boundary is part of a word, not a break", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L191-L195", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The reader emits one run per style, so [link](x). is three runs and one word; splitting each run on its own spaces and rejoining with a single space put a gap before every full stop that followed a link. Words are built across runs first, then laid out."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_html_void_elements", "label": "Void elements are checked before hidden tags", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L197-L200", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "An HTML void element must never be in the hidden-tag set. <meta> was, and since nothing ever closes it, every page with a charset declaration rendered as a blank screen; the two lists are now separate and the void one is checked first."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_diff_frame", "label": "diff_frame gives drawing and hit-testing one geometry", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L202-L211", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "An editable diff is a mapping rather than a second editor: a diff row carries the line number it shows and the buffer's cursor is measured in the same line numbers, which is enough to place the caret. One shared geometry, because an editable diff means a click has to land on the character it looks like it landed on."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_diff_tab_ownership", "label": "A working file's diff carries the buffer and takes over its tab", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L243-L250", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Both follow from the diff's right-hand column being that buffer: two tabs would mean two undo histories for one file, and a diff that pointed at a tab would have to be put back together every time focus moved. It also retires the workaround where the diff closed on a tab switch because its keystrokes would otherwise go to whatever buffer was now in front."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_commit_diff_tab", "label": "A commit's diff outlives the panel that opened it", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L257-L260", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A commit diff carries no buffer, so hiding it removes the tab outright rather than handing a file back; a tab outlives the thing that opened it, which is most of what being a tab means."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_active_buffer_tab_check", "label": "Preview and image decoder check the tab in front is a file", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L252-L255", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Both work off the active buffer and a diff tab has one, so without the check, diffing a Markdown file left a rendered preview behind it and diffing a PNG drew the picture over the diff."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_completion_triggers", "label": "Two bounded completion signals", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L213-L221", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Trigger characters come from the server's own initialize reply rather than a list of ours, because which characters mean 'a member follows' is a fact about the language; they arrive one character at a time, which is also how a keystroke does, so > covers ->. The other signal is two characters of a word and fires once rather than once per letter. Neither signal starts a server: a keystroke must not spawn a process."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_hover_contentformat", "label": "initialize declares hover.contentFormat Markdown", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L223-L228", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The hover popup is the Markdown preview in a box, and without hover.contentFormat a server must send plaintext - clangd did, so a signature arrived as a flat string with nothing to colour. The reply is kept as written rather than flattened to one line, which is what the status bar used to need."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_hover_dwell", "label": "The dwell is fenced in on every side", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L230-L234", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "It is the one thing the editor does without being asked: only over text, only after the pointer has been still, only once per rest, and only when a server is already running. It schedules itself through the same deadline the caret blink and the autosave use, so an idle window still waits for nothing at all."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_workspace_tabs", "label": "Workspace holds Vec<Tab> and active() returns Option<&Editor>", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L236-L241", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A tab is a file, a diff, or the settings page, and None for a tab that is not editing anything is the same answer an empty workspace already gave - which is the reason every call site already handled it, and what kept this from touching the hundred places that ask for the active buffer."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_page_hit_testing", "label": "Full-area pages are hit-tested where the text area is", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L262-L266", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Not before the chrome: testing the settings page first meant it returned 'nothing here' for every click outside its own switches, which swallowed the sidebar and, once it became a tab, the click on its own tab. The landing page had the same shape and the same fix."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_settings_unknown_keys", "label": "The settings file tolerates unknown keys", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L268-L272", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "deny_unknown_fields was right while the set only grew; the first time a key was retired it took every other preference in the file down with it, because the whole parse failed and fell back to defaults. Unknown keys are now logged and ignored, which still catches a typo without punishing history."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_html_preview_removed", "label": "The HTML preview is gone", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L274-L278", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A reader that understands the structural tags but no stylesheet, script or layout renders a document the way nothing on the web would, and 'roughly a browser' is a worse promise than no promise. Inline HTML inside Markdown stayed, because there it is part of the Markdown rather than a claim to render a page."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_line_spans", "label": "line_spans is the one definition of the lines a cursor is on", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L280-L284", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "It merges overlaps and decides that a selection ending exactly at a line start has not reached into that line. Delete, duplicate, move and comment all share it, so they cannot disagree about what the current line is when a selection spans three of them."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_move_lines", "label": "move_lines takes one cursor only", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L286-L288", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Deliberately narrower than the other multi-cursor commands: with several cursors the blocks can meet in the middle and the result is nobody's idea of a swap."}, {"id": "docs_architecture_toggle_comment", "label": "toggle_comment makes one decision for the whole block", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md", "source_location": "L288-L291", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "If every line is commented it uncomments, else it comments all of it - because line by line turns a half-commented block into its own inverse, which is never what was meant."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_architecture_performance_invariants", "target": "docs_architecture_redraw_only_on_damage", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source